$ =========================================================================
$ cessna210_flagship_body_cruciform.bdf — Step 66 body-panel OVERLAY for the
$ Cessna 210 flagship SOL 144 family.
$
$ NOT A STANDALONE DECK.  This file carries only the fuselage aerodynamics; it
$ is INCLUDEd *after* sample/cessna210_flagship_bulk.bdf by a driver deck:
$
$     SOL 144
$     ...case control...
$     INCLUDE 'cessna210_flagship_bulk.bdf'
$     INCLUDE 'cessna210_flagship_body_cruciform.bdf'
$
$ The flagship bulk is left BYTE-IDENTICAL by design: every Step 65 anchor
$ (CL, CZa, Cma, elevator deg/g, divergence margin, MONPNT1 = n*W) stays valid
$ and this overlay is purely additive.  The bulk reserves CAERO1 EIDs 6000/7000
$ for exactly this purpose.
$
$ CRUCIFORM variant: the panels carry PAERO1 10, so they are ordinary VLM
$ surfaces — horseshoe vortices, wakes, and full AIC coupling to the wing and
$ tail.  That coupling is real and measurable: adding these panels UNCORRECTED
$ already moves the airplane's Cm0 (see the strip variant, which cannot).
$
$ Coordinate system: X = freestream (forward), Y = starboard, Z = up; origin at
$ the nose.  Units: SI (m, N, kg, Pa).  Full-span model.
$ =========================================================================
$
$ ------------------------------------------------------- BODY PANEL GEOMETRY
$ sbeam has no slender-body element, so the fuselage is a CRUCIFORM of two
$ crossing flat surfaces:
$   * horizontal panel (CAERO1 6000, XY plane, normal +Z) — body lift / pitch;
$   * vertical   panel (CAERO1 7000, XZ plane, normal +Y) — body sideforce / yaw.
$
$ PLACEMENT.  Both panels terminate at x=5.00, well ahead of the VTP LE (6.60)
$ and HTP LE (6.90), and both sit low — the horizontal at z=0.30 (below the HTP
$ at 0.70), the vertical wholly below the VTP root (z<0.60, in the same y=0
$ plane as the fin).  So no body box, and no +X trailing leg, reaches the
$ empennage station.  That matters here: a cruciform box
$ genuinely loads its neighbours, so an overlap would be a numerical artefact.
$
$ MESH.  Both panels are 16/32 boxes with box aspect ratios inside the VLM's
$ [0.5, 2.0] band (0.73 each).  The vertical panel is 2 spanwise x 16 chordwise
$ rather than the 4x8 of the retired cessna210_body.bdf: 4 boxes up a 0.40 m
$ height gave AR 0.17 and tripped the high-AR kernel warning.  Two z-stations
$ still give the arm spread needed to separate roll from yaw.
$
$ ID NOTE: box IDs run 6000-6015 and 7000-7031.  Those numerically overlap the
$ flagship's overlay CONM2 EIDs (6001-6014, 6020) — different namespaces, no
$ conflict, but do not read one for the other.
$
CAERO1, 6000, 10, 0, 2, 8, 0, 0, 1
+, 0.30, -0.40, 0.30, 4.70, 0.30, 0.40, 0.30, 4.70
CAERO1, 7000, 10, 0, 2, 16, 0, 0, 1
+, 0.30, 0.0, 0.05, 4.70, 0.30, 0.0, 0.45, 4.70
$
$ ------------------------------------------------------------------ SPLINING
$ SPLINE0 = no structural coupling: body elasticity is negligible, so the
$ panels add no deformation.  Field 5 names the master GRID that carries their
$ resultant into the structure (Step 64).  GRID 900 is the trim reference,
$ spliced into the fuselage CBAR chain and already listed in SET1 1310, so the
$ injected body load lands inside the whole-aircraft MONPNT3 cut.  Naming it
$ explicitly rather than defaulting to the SUPORT grid documents the intent
$ (here they are the same grid).
SPLINE0, 9400, 6000, 6000, 6015, 900
SPLINE0, 9500, 7000, 7000, 7031, 900
$
$ ------------------------------------------------------- MONITOR (ALL 372 BOXES)
$ The bulk's AELIST 1400 covers the 324 FLYING boxes only.  With body panels
$ active that monitor no longer returns n*W — it falls short by exactly the body
$ resultant.  This adds the whole-airplane collection so the aero-only = n*W
$ identity still holds; keeping BOTH makes the shortfall measurable.
AELIST, 1401, 1000, THRU, 1095, 2000, THRU, 2095, 3000, THRU, 3047
+, 4000, THRU, 4047, 5000, THRU, 5035, 6000, THRU, 6015
+, 7000, THRU, 7031
AECOMP, ALLAERB, AELIST, 1401
MONPNT1, MALLARB, AIRCRAFT AERO INC BODY, 35, ALLAERB, 0, 2.4375, 0.0, 0.60
$
